Pax Connect licensing overview
==============================

Pax Connect is made of separate parts under different licenses. This file is
a summary; the license texts linked below are what actually apply.

| Path | License | Text |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `macos/` (the AppKit app) | PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0 | `macos/LICENSE` |
| `api/` (gRPC / protobuf definitions) | MIT | `api/LICENSE` |
| Everything else, including `daemon/`, `patches/`, `scripts/`, `rulesets/`, `docs/` | GPL-3.0 | `third_party/licenses/GPL-3.0.txt` |

Third-party components keep their own licenses: see THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.
The name "Pax Connect" and the app icon are covered by TRADEMARKS.md, not by
any of the licenses above.

Noncommercial use of the app
----------------------------
The app (`macos/`) is source-available, not open source. Noncommercial use is
free. Commercial use of the app requires a separate license from the copyright
holder. To ask for one, open an issue at
https://github.com/Moore-developers/pax-connect-app/issues.

The GPL parts
-------------
The Go daemon and core link sing-box (GPL-3.0-or-later), so they are, and must
stay, GPL-3.0. Anyone may use those parts commercially under the GPL terms;
that cannot be restricted. The noncommercial restriction applies only to the
app in `macos/`.

How the parts fit together
--------------------------
The app and the GPL daemon/core are separate programs. They talk over gRPC on
a Unix socket and are distributed together in one release package as an
aggregate (GPL-3.0 section 5). The GPL does not extend to the app, and the app
license does not extend to the GPL programs. The protocol definitions in
`api/` are MIT so that both sides can use them.
